+- **The gap is not always a missing surface — it can be a missing LEG of a surface you already cover.**
+  169 → 172 asked "which surface *family* has this signal?". Item 173 (2026-07-29) found the same signal
+  missing along a different axis entirely: `APY_SANITY_LIMIT` was enforced on `audit-app.js`'s two
+  **non-rendered** legs (`:323` text-surface prescan, `:1010`/`:1017` pool-snapshot prescan) and on
+  **neither** rendered one — the rendered leg had only a magnitude rule (`1e11`) tuned to the previous bug,
+  so a figure 36× the rail sailed through the last check standing between a bad number and a human's eyes.
+  **The probe is one line and worth running on every detector you own:** `grep -n <RAIL_CONSTANT> <detector>`
+  and ask whether every leg that *renders or reads* the guarded field appears in the output. A constant used
+  in some legs reads as "enforced" at a glance — that glance is the bug.
+- **Two checks over the same field are not redundant when they answer different questions.** Magnitude
+  ("is this number impossibly big?") and rail-relative ("is this number bigger than what the product itself
+  calls credible?") are independent; neither subsumes the other, and lowering the magnitude floor to cover
+  the rail case would false-positive on every legitimate large `$` figure. Add the second rule; do not retune
+  the first.
+- **A new signal that finds nothing must prove it CAN find something on the real thing.** 173's true negative
+  was proven with an injection that reaches the screen through a live render path
+  (`kpis.apyMomentum = 5000` → `PoolDetail.js`'s rate-momentum note, on a pool the product does not
+  anomaly-flag), not with a fixture. When you look for such an injection, look for the field the rails reach
+  only *transitively* — 173's positive control exists precisely because `apyMomentum` is bounded only by its
+  series endpoints, while every directly-railed field was unreachable.
+
